INSERT INTO posts VALUES(1,'What is Ace?','what-is-ace',1,replace('# Pricing\n- Ace thrive''s w/ any Node library, so there''s no necessity to use our [standard plugins](#plugins) but if you do, please know that `some` plugins have costs\n- Ace gains `no revenue` on top of these costs\n- For perspective, please feel free to review the pricing of **[Convex](https://www.convex.dev/pricing)** & **[Vercel](https://vercel.com/pricing)**, the **top shelf** providers in our space!\n\n\n<!--{\n  "$component": "Lottie",\n  "src": "/lottie/coins.lottie",\n  "$div": { "class": "coins" },\n  "$canvas": { "class": "good-rising" }\n}-->\n\n\n:::table-pricing\n| Feature               | Cost   | How |\n| ----------------------| -------| -------|\n| 💜 **[SQL Database](https://turso.tech/pricing)** reads per month    | `500 Million` for free | [🔗](/post/database) |\n| Database writes per month   | `10 Million` for free |\n| Database storage  | `5GB` for free |\n| 💙 **[File](https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/pricing/)** reads per month  | `10 million` for free | [🔗](/post/file-uploads) |\n| File writes per month | `1 million` for free |\n| File storage          | `10GB` for free |\n| File bandwidth | `Free` |\n| 💚 **[Web Socket](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/)** requests per day | `100,000` for free | [🔗](/post/ace-live-server) |\n| Web socket cost when connected but idle | `Free`|  |\n| 🧡 **[Emails](https://www.brevo.com/pricing/)** per day | `300` for free | [🔗](/post/brevo) |\n| 💛 **[Serverless](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/)** requests per day | `100,000` for free | [🔗](/post/deploy) |\n| ❤️ **Per developer cost**     | `Free` | ❤️ |\n:::\n\n\n\n## What is Ace?\n- ❤️ **`Optimal`** code for Web Developers!\n- All our code is **`free`**, [MIT Licensed](https://github.com/orgs/acets-team/repositories) & grouped into **plugins**!\n- To use a plugin, just set it to **true** in your [ace.config.js](/post/ace-config)! 🙏\n\n\n\n## Plugins\n  1. **[Solid](https://docs.solidjs.com/)**: Enjoy dev friendly `HMR`, remarkable `SSR` & optimal `DOM` updates w/ `signals`\n  1. **[Drizzle](https://orm.drizzle.team/)**: Perform type-safe `Database` queries w/ a beautiful updating data `UI`\n  1. **[Turso](https://turso.tech/)**: Create lightweight `SQL` databases, that scale to `millions` of instances\n  1. **[Cloudflare](https://www.cloudflare.com/)**: The best `Serverless`, `Websockets` & `File` Storage Solution Ever\n  1. **[AgGrid](https://www.ag-grid.com/)**: Add Solid Components w/in `scrollable, filterable & sortable` tables\n  1. **[Valibot](https://valibot.dev/guides/comparison/)**: Enjoy Zod features in a `modular` library\n  1. **[Markdown-It](https://markdown-it.github.io/markdown-it/)**: Create `SEO` Friendly and/or `Dynamically` Generated `HTML` from `Markdown`\n  1. **[Highlight.js](https://github.com/highlightjs/highlight.js)**: Highlight code in your `Markdown` w/ `100''s` of supported `languages`\n  1. **[Lottie](https://lottiefiles.com/featured-free-animations)**: Browse, Create and Download lovely `lightweight` animations\n  1. **[Charts.js](https://www.chartjs.org/)**: Create `Line, Bar, Donut, Area, Polar & Radar` charts w/ ease\n  1. **[Brevo](https://www.brevo.com/)**: Easily create & send beautiful email `templates`\n\n\n\n## Create an Ace App\n- Bash:\n    ```bash\n    npx create-ace-app@latest\n    ```\n- 🚨 When opening `Create Ace App` **locally** for the first time after an `npm run dev`, it will take 5-10 seconds to load 😡 b/c Vite is altering code to optimize [`HMR`](https://vite.dev/guide/features#hot-module-replacement) (so subsequent loads are instant 🤓)\n- This slow initial load is **no factor** in production & to prove this, here''s [Create Ace App In Production](https://create-ace-app.jquery-ssr.workers.dev)! 🚀 Deployed to Cloudflare Workers via `git push`, deploy directions [here](#deploy)!\n    ![Create Ace App in Production](https://i.imgur.com/UcfHhXh.jpeg)\n- Desktop App install is now ready! (works on `localhost` too btw!)\n    ![Create Desktop Application](https://i.imgur.com/iN3ywWW.png)\n- Any cookie you want can be shared between your site & app, phone install''s work too and when you do a `git push` to deploy your site, all your app''s update automatically! ❤️\n\n\n\n\n\n## Why Was Ace Created?\nThanks to failure, I am very sensitive to performance. In college I stumbled upon a plugin that could turn a Wordpress site into a Social Network! I thought this was remarkable, so I created a Facebook event entitled `Runner''s Social Network` and said in 2 weeks, I will drop the link to Instride! 2 weeks later over 2,000 people were attending this event!\n\nAloha, my name is Christopher Carrington, thanks for being here btw!\n\nSo 2 weeks later I post the link 🎉 and minutes later Instride is `unusable` 😱 Too much load for shared hosting.\n\nSo now I''m on the phone with GoDaddy learning about `virtual private servers`, and how it''ll take 1-2 days to migrate the `files`, `database` & `DNS` 🤬 So I take the site down, complete the migration to an over `$100` a month server, notify customers we''re back and never surpass `30%` of this new servers RAM b/c I bought too much and the traffic was never the same as day 1.\n\nBurned & learned but this was 15 years ago, lets fast forward to today. I''m unemployed and I say okay, time to learn what people want. So after 9 years of `AnguarJS`, `Vue` & `Svelte` I decide to learn` Next.JS`. Long story short (too late) I love their functional components but I want more from a performance perspective. Components rerunning on every state update is not my favorite architecture.\n\nSo I decide to not just learn for a job but to learn what aligns with what I care about, so I start learning Solid & I fall in love w/ it! From a performance & simplicity perspective, Solid is top shelf! But then I start workin on a couple different projects (a site for my Mom & a site for my friend) and I want to share code between these sites so I create a folder called `fundamentals` to share functions, classes & types between these projects. This is how `Ace` began & each one of our fundamentals aligns w/ 3 pillars. `Performance`, `Simplicity` & `Cost`!\n\n1. `Performance:`\n    - @ build time:\n        - Like `Hono`, we generate a `tree` to all api routes, to allow API resolution to be type-safe & fast!\n        - We generate the `segments` to every route and api, so when we create URL''s there is no regex required & it''s as fast as possible!\n    - We align w/ `Cloudflare`, the performance masters, b/c I love their: \n        - [Optimal isolates](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/reference/how-workers-works/) that remove virtual machine cold starts\n        - Brilliant [hibernating websockets](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets) that when idle, remain open & cost nothing\n        - & [generous pricing](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing/) that allows 100,000 request per day for free!\n    - Because we are built w/ Solid, we have access to all Solid features, including their fantastic `createSignal()`!\n1. `Simplicity:`\n    - When we change anything we want to know how this updates things everywhere! To accomplish this Ace has first class typescript support & ensure all is `type-safe` but Typescript does not check every file on every save so technically there can be bugs in files on a save that we don''t know about. So we''ve got `npm run typesafe` for this, which does a full project scan and then the compiler throws any errors in the VsCode `Problems` tab! Typescript does scan quite a bit on save but just to be sure, we''ve got backup!\n    - Imagine using a new code base and every function is not only type-safe but includes `JSDoc` comments that explain the what & they why of everything! This is our standard!\n    - If you''d to create love `one` code base that works as an `ios` app, `android` app, `desktop` app & `website` then Ace is perfect for you! `Push notifications` on lock screens work for all devices, any cookie you want can be shared between em all & on `git push` updates flow to all automatically! With Ace, this complexity, is simple!\n    - Cloudflare has revolutionized `real-time` data w/ their hibernating websockets & w/ Ace flowing database updates to customers w/ no browser refresh required is beautifully simple!\n    - Dates can be tricky, ms''s here, seconds there, and it''s lovely to have a suite of date helpers to ease the process of working w/ dates!\n    - Aria compliant components is tricky all our components are! Toasts, modals & much more, all included!\n1. `Cost:`\n    - Cloudflare is best in class, we''ve already gone over that but so is Turso! 500 million monthly reads for free is too much for me to comprehend, but the perfect amount for me to appreciate and we will continue to have first class support for their lovely offering! \n\nI can keep going but you get the point! When we add a plugin to Ace it''s becuase that company embodies these pillars & I **`promise`** to continue evolving Ace, to grow w/ industries leaders, showcase their lovely products & provide optimal web fundamentals!','\n',char(10)),2,1765490274000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(2,'CLI','cli',2,replace('# CLI\n- There are 2 different ways to use the `ace` cli. To use @:\n    1. `package.json > scripts` please do `npm i @acets-team/ace`\n    1. `Bash Terminal` please do `npm i @acets-team/ace -g`\n\n\n\n## `ace build <env>`\n- When we run `ace build local` the text after `ace build` is the current environment so in this example the current environment is `local` & with `ace build prod` the current environment is `prod`\n- Because this command is done @ build time, we have access to this variable @ compile time & @ runtime, on the `FE` or `BE`, example:\n  ```ts\n  import { env } from ''@ace/env''\n\n  console.log(env) // local\n  ```\n- Reads from the `ace.config.js` the:\n    - `plugins`, to ensure the plugins we set to `true` are available in our `.ace` folder\n    - `api` directory & then builds:\n        - 4 api `trees` (`GET`, `POST`, `DELETE`, `PUT`) that aligns `pathname segments` to an `api name` which is used in the `api map` to give us fast api resolution\n        - An `api map` to point `api names` (ex: `apiGetProfiles`) to the proper modules to give us `type-safety`\n        - URL segments for each route so that creating urls is as fast as possible & requires no regex \n    - `app` directory (contains routes and layouts)\n        - `<Router />` components to be used @  `app.tsx` via `createApp()` example:\n          ```ts\n          import ''./app.css''\n          import ''./post/post.css''\n          import ''./search/Search.css''\n          import ''@ace/tron.styles.css''\n          import ''@ace/tabs.styles.css''\n          import ''@ace/modal.styles.css''\n          import ''@ace/toast.styles.css''\n          import ''@ace/pulse.styles.css''\n          import ''@ace/loading.styles.css''\n          import ''@ace/tooltip.styles.css''\n          import { createApp } from ''@ace/createApp''\n          import { StoreProvider } from ''@src/store/store''\n\n\n          export default createApp([StoreProvider])\n          ```\n        - A `route tree` that aligns `pathname segments` to a `full pathname` which is used in the `route map` to give us fast route resolution\n        - An `route map` to point `route paths` (ex: `/example/:id`) to the proper route module to give us `type-safety`\n        - URL segments for each route so that creating urls is as fast as possible & requires no regex \n- 🚨 `ace build` creates a lot of types & modules for us, and the majority will dynamically update while we make edits but there are some changes that require a fresh `ace build` in order to take effect, these include when:\n    - An `API`, `Route` or `Layout` is `created` or `deleted`\n    - A `Layout` is `added` or `removed` from a `new Route()` or `new Route404()`''s `.layout([])`\n\n\n\n## `ace sw`\n- Keeps `app` version (within `package.json`) & `cache` version (within `sw.js`) **aligned**\n    - Reads the `version` from `package.json` and adds it into `public/sw.js`\n    - Looks for a line in `sw.js` that starts w/ `const packageDotJsonVersion =` and adds the `version` there!\n- Adds `public/.ace/swAddOffLineSupport.js` so that it may be used @ `public/sw.js`\n\n\n\n## `ace-password`\n- Helpful when creating secrets, unique identifiers, or tokens\n- With WebCrypto Api, creates a random sequence of `64 random bytes` and then encodes them into a `URL-safe Base64 string`\n- The number of possible unique values for a 64-byte token is `2^512`. This number is astronomically large\n- Generally considered extremely resistant to brute-force and birthday attacks\n','\n',char(10)),2,1765490338000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(3,'Ace Config','ace-config',3,replace('# Ace Config\n- **Filename:** `ace.config.js`\n- **Folder:** Same as `package.json`\n\n\n\n## Example:\n```ts\n// @ts-check \n\n/** @type {import(''@acets-team/ace'').AceConfig} */\nexport const config = {\n  sw: true,\n  apiDir: ''src/api'',\n  appDir: ''src/app'',\n  logCaughtErrors: true,\n  origins: {\n    prod: ''https://create-ace-app.jquery-ssr.workers.dev'',\n    local: [''http://localhost:3000'', ''http://localhost:3001'']\n  },\n  plugins: {\n    hljs: true,\n    solid: true,\n    turso: true,\n    agGrid: true,\n    valibot: true,\n    chartjs: true,\n    markdownIt: true,\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## Prop Details:\n\n```ts\nexport type AceConfig = {\n  /** The directory that holds your api files */\n  apiDir?: string,\n  /** The directory that holds your routes and layouts */\n  appDir?: string,\n  /** Ace is a set of helpful files we call fundamentals. Fundamentals are grouped by plugins. Set a plugin to true to gain access to the fundamentals it holds. */\n  plugins: PluginsConfig,\n  /** Default is `tsconfig.json`, helps us resolve aliases */\n  tsConfigPath?: string,\n  /** \n   * - The `key` is the `env` which is set w/ the build command\n   * - Ex: `ace build local`, in this example the key is `local`. The value is a `string` or `array` of `strings` which are the allowed origins\n   * - 🚨 If your origin is ''*'' cookies won''t work, not an Ace limitation, that''s just how HTTP work :)\n   */\n  origins: Record<string, string | string[]>\n  /** \n   * - When set to true \n   * - On build => IF `config.sw` = `true` THEN the file in your `public` directory that starts w/ `sw` and is a `.js` will have the `const packageDotJsonVersion =` updated w/in the file\n   * - Helps maintain alignment between app versions and cache versions\n   * \n  */\n  sw?: boolean\n  /**\n   * - Optional, defaults to `_info`\n   * - Messages are grouped by name: `Map<string, Signal<string[]>>`\n   * - If `response.error.message` is defined, we''ll put that value @ `scope.mesages[defaultMessageName]`\n   */\n  defaultMessageName?: string\n  /**\n   * - Optional, defaults to `❌ Sorry but an error just happened`\n   * - If no other error message is provided we''ll show this\n   */\n  defaultError?: string\n  /**\n   * - The key aligns w/ the keys @ `ace.config.js` > `origins`\n   * - The value is the host url to the Ace Live Server when @ that `origin`\n   * - 🚨 Host meaning no `http://` example: `liveHosts: { local: ''localhost:8787'', prod: ''live.example.com'' }`\n   */\n  liveHosts?: Record<string, string>\n  /** Would you like to log errors */\n  logCaughtErrors?: boolean,\n  /** \n   * - Optional, Specify an `id` and the `path` to a folder of markdowns from the current working directory\n   * - When defined the `mdFolders` will get a `parseMarkdownFolders()` helper created at build time\n   */\n  mdFolders?: { id: string, path: string }[]\n}\n```\n\n## Plugins\n- All our code is **`free`**, [MIT Licensed](https://github.com/orgs/acets-team/repositories) & grouped into **plugins**!\n- To use a plugin, just set it to **true** in your [ace.config.js](/post/ace-config)! 🙏\n  ```ts\n  export type PluginsConfig = {\n    /**\n     * Enables **SolidJS** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     *\n     * Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     * - `solid-js`\n     * - `@solidjs/meta`\n     * - `@solidjs/start`\n     * - `@solidjs/router`\n     * \n     * @link https://docs.solidjs.com/\n     */\n    solid?: boolean\n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **Valibot** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     *\n     * Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     * - `valibot`\n     * \n     * @link https://valibot.dev/guides/comparison/\n     */\n    valibot?: boolean\n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **Turso** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     *\n     * Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     * - `@libsql/client`\n     * - `drizzle-orm`\n     * - `drizzle-kit`\n     * \n     * @link https://turso.tech/\n     */\n    turso?: boolean\n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **Markdown** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     * \n     * - Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     *     - `markdown-it`\n     *\n     * @link https://www.npmjs.com/package/markdown-it\n     */\n    markdownIt?: boolean\n  \n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **Markdown** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     * \n     * - Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     *     - `highlight.js`\n     *     - `@highlightjs/cdn-assets`\n     *\n     * @link https://highlightjs.readthedocs.io/\n     */\n    hljs?: boolean\n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **Cloudflare** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     * \n     * - Helpful when using `Ace Live Server`\n     * - Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     *     - `wrangler`\n     *     - `@cloudflare/workers-types`\n     *\n     * @link https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/\n     */\n    cf?: boolean\n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **Lottie** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     * \n     * - Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     *     - `@lottiefiles/dotlottie-web`\n     *\n     * @link https://lottiefiles.com/featured-free-animations\n     */\n    lottie?: boolean\n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **AgGrid** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     * \n     * - Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     *     - `ag-grid-community`\n     * @link https://www.ag-grid.com/\n     */\n    agGrid?: boolean\n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **Chart.js** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     * \n     * - Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     *     - `chart.js`\n     *\n     * @link https://www.chartjs.org/\n     */\n    chartjs?: boolean\n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **Brevo** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     * \n     * - Requires the following `.env` variable:\n     *     - `process.env.BREVO_API_KEY`\n     *\n     * @link https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/209467485-Create-and-manage-your-API-keys\n     * @link https://developers.brevo.com/docs/send-a-transactional-email\n     * @link https://developers.brevo.com/docs/how-it-works\n     * @link https://www.cloudflare.com/en-gb/ips/\n     */\n    brevo?: boolean\n  \n    /**\n     * Enables **Zod** fundamentals (helpful `@ace/` modules)\n     *\n     * Requires the following npm dev imports:\n     * - `zod`\n     * \n     * @link https://zod.dev/\n     */\n    zod?: boolean\n  }\n  ```','\n',char(10)),2,1765490338000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(4,'Environment Information','environment-information',4,replace('# Environment Information\n- It''s nice to code in different environements, ex: `local`, `dev`, `prod`\n- At `run-time` it''s lovely to know what environment we''re in & the current value of variables provided by `Ace` that are dependent on the environement\n\n\n\n## `env`\n- When we run `ace build local` the text after `ace build` is the current environment so in this example the current environment is `local` & with `ace build prod` the current environment is `prod`\n- Because this command is done @ build time, we have access to this variable @ `run-time`, on the `FE` (frontend) or `BE` (backed), example:\n  ```ts\n  import { env } from ''@ace/env''\n\n  console.log(env) // local | prod\n  ```\n\n\n\n## `buildOrigin`\n- Available via:\n  ```ts\n  import { buildOrigin } from ''@ace/env''\n\n  console.log(env) // https://example.com\n  ```\n- Ideal for:\n    - Build time url generation (ex: Urls created on the `FE` to `Routes`, `Apis` or w/in open graph `meta tags`)\n- Unite `ace.config.js` and `ace build <env>`, example:\n  ```ts\n  // ace.config.js\n  {\n    origins: {\n      local: [''http://localhost:3000'', ''http://localhost:3001'']\n      prod: [''https://acets.org'', ''https://www.acets.org''],\n    }\n  }\n  ```\n- 🚨 IF the current  `ace.config.js` > `origins` > `env` has more then 1 origin defined, the first origin will be the `buildOrigin`, so in the example above:\n    - for `local` the `buildOrigin` will be `http://localhost:3000`\n    - & for `prod` the `buildOrigin` will be `https://acets.org`\n\n\n\n## `scope.requestUrlOrigin`\n- Ideal for:\n    - Creating absolute run time urls on the `BE` that match the `active server` (ex: magic link url)\n    - CORS Validation (ensure `requestUrlOrigin` matches allowed `configOrigins`)\n- Available from [`ScopeBE`](/post/ace-config)\n- The origin of the `current HTTP request URL`\n- Helps us:\n    - Know where the current request was received\n\n\n\n## `configOrigins`\n- Available via \n  ```ts\n  import { configOrigins } from ''@ace/env''\n\n  console.log(configOrigins) // new Set([''https://example.com'', ''https://www.example.com''])\n  ```\n- Ideal for:\n    - Validating incoming request origins (ex: during CORS checks)\n- Aligns  [`ace.config.js`](/post/ace-config) > `origins` w/ the current `ace build <env>`\n- The resulting `Set` tells us, all the origins we support for the current `environment` @ build time\n','\n',char(10)),2,1765509237000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(5,'Atoms','atoms',5,replace('# Atoms\n- Thankfully Ace is powered by [Solid](https://docs.solidjs.com/reference/basic-reactivity/create-signal), so the optimal `createSignal()` is always available! 🔮\n- Atoms are `signals`\n- Solid''s `createSignal()` is best if your dynamic data is confined to one component \n- `Atoms` are best if you''d love your dynamic data to flow betwen multiple components, flow between multiple browser tabs or persist beyond a browser refresh\n- An Atoms `save` props sets the Atom''s save `location` to `memory` (m), `session storage` (ss), `local storage` (ls) or `index db` (idb)! 💾\n- An Atoms `is` prop helps us know how to serialize and deserialze from the save location\n- Setting custom `onSerialize` / `onDeserialze` @ `new Atom()` is avaialble!\n\n\n## Storage Options\n\n:::table-atoms\n| Feature                    | m     | ss     | ls     | idb   |\n| ---------------------------| ------| ------ | -------| ------|\n| Available in Any Component | ✅    | ✅      | ✅     | ✅    |\n| Persist on Refresh         | ❌    | ✅      | ✅     | ✅    |\n| Persist on Tab Close       | ❌    | ❌      | ✅     | ✅    |\n| Persist if Offline         | ❌    | ❌      | ✅     | ✅    |\n| Share Across Tabs          | ❌    | ❌      | ✅     | ✅    |\n| Supports Async             | ❌    | ❌      | ❌     | ✅    |\n| Size Limit                 | v8Max | 5–10MB | 5–10MB | 100MB |\n:::\n\n\n\n## Create Atoms\n\n```ts\n// src/store/atoms.ts\n\n\nimport { Atom } from ''@ace/atom''\nimport { createAtoms } from ''@ace/createAtoms''\nimport { AceMarkdownHeading } from ''@ace/aceMarkdown''\nimport type { InferAtoms, ApiName2Either } from ''@ace/types''\n\n\n// these are the atoms that power this site! 🤔\n// on this site, the searchQuery & searchResults persist through refresh\n// this is happening b/c of the searchQuery & searchResults Atoms below\n\n\nexport const { atoms, useAtoms, AtomsContext } = createAtoms({\n  searchQuery: new Atom({ save: ''idb'', init: '''' }),\n  isSidenavVisible: new Atom({ save: ''m'', init: false }),\n  headings: new Atom<AceMarkdownHeading[]>({ save: ''m'', is: ''json'', init: [] }),\n\n  // ApiName2Either is type-safe! 👷‍♀️ { data?: T, error?: AceResError }\n  // It knows your current api''s, so ''apiGetPartners'' is type-safe!\n  // & it knows this Api''s response type, so this Atom is now type-safe too!\n  partners: new Atom<ApiName2Either<''apiGetPartners''>>({ save: ''idb'', is: ''json'' }),\n  searchResults: new Atom<ApiName2Either<''apiSearch''>[''data'']>({ save: ''idb'', is: ''json'' }),\n})\n\n\n// Atoms are placed into a Solid Store 🔮 and this is its type\nexport type BaseAtoms = InferAtoms<typeof atoms>\n```\n\n\n\n## Create AtomsProvider\n\n```ts\n// src/store/AtomsProvider.ts\n\nimport { atoms, AtomsContext } from ''./atoms''\nimport { createAtomsProvider } from ''@ace/createAtomsProvider''\n\n\nexport const AtomsProvider = createAtomsProvider({\n  atoms,\n  AtomsContext,\n})\n```\n\n\n\n## Set AtomsProvider\n```ts\n// src/app.tsx\n\nimport ''./app.css'' // 🚨 vite requires local css paths to be relative\nimport ''./post/post.css''\nimport ''./_Search/Search.css''\nimport ''@ace/base.styles.css''\nimport ''@ace/tron.styles.css''\nimport ''@ace/tabs.styles.css''\nimport ''@ace/modal.styles.css''\nimport ''@ace/toast.styles.css''\nimport ''@ace/pulse.styles.css''\nimport ''@ace/loading.styles.css''\nimport ''@ace/tooltip.styles.css''\nimport ''@ace/dropdown.styles.css''\nimport { BaseApp } from ''@ace/baseApp''\nimport { AtomsProvider } from ''@src/store/AtomsProvider''\n\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return <>\n    <AtomsProvider>\n      <BaseApp />\n    </AtomsProvider>\n  </>\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## `useAtm()` Properties\n- `store`\n  - [Solid''s store](https://docs.solidjs.com/concepts/stores) is the accessor to all our store values\n  - Each `Atom` is added into the store and is accessible via it''s `key`. So based on the example atoms above, we can access:\n      - `store.count`\n      - `store.newsletterForm.name`\n- `setStore()`\n  - [Solid''s setStore()](https://docs.solidjs.com/concepts/stores) helps us to assign store value, examples:\n    - `setStore(''count'', 1)`\n    - `setStore(''count'', c => c + 1)`\n    - `setStore(''newsletterForm'', ''name'', ''Chris'')`\n    - `setStore(''newsletterForm'', {name: ''Chris'', email: ''chris@gmail.com''})`\n- `save()`\n  - Accepts a store key, and will `save` to the Atom''s save location, example:\n      - `save(''count'')`\n      - `save(''newsletterForm'')`\n- `set()`\n  - Calls [Solid setStore()](https://docs.solidjs.com/concepts/stores) and then calls `save()`, examples:\n      - `set(''count'', 1)`\n      - `set(''count'', c => c + 1)`\n      - `set(''newsletterForm'', ''name'', ''Chris'')`\n      - `set(''newsletterForm'', {name: ''Chris'', email: ''chris@gmail.com''})`\n- `sync()`\n  - Calls [Solid''s reconcile()](https://docs.solidjs.com/concepts/stores) which efficiently diffs and merges entire objects or arrays and then calls `save()`, examples:\n    ```ts\n    // array example:\n\n    store.users = [ // current users\n      { _id: ''1'', name: ''Chris'', active: true },\n      { _id: ''2'', name: ''Alex'', active: false },\n    ]\n\n    const newUsers = [ // updated api users\n      { _id: ''1'', name: ''Chris'', active: false }, // changed\n      { _id: ''2'', name: ''Alex'', active: false },  // identical\n    ]\n\n    // Only Chris''s active property triggers reactive updates\n    // key is optional, if undefined the key is ''id''\n    sync(''users'', newUsers, { key: ''_id'' })\n\n\n    // object example:\n\n    store.user = {\n      _id: ''123'',\n      profile: {\n        name: ''Chris'',\n        stats: { posts: 9, followers: 27 },\n      },\n    }\n\n    const newUser = {\n      profile: { stats: { followers: 30 } },\n    }\n\n    sync(''user'', newUser)\n    ```\n','\n',char(10)),2,1765510625000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(6,'Ref (Directives)','ref',6,replace('# Ref (Directives)\n- Accessing `html elements` is a typical developer necessity. This helps us:\n    - Call functions on these elements like `element.foucs()`\n    - Or attach listners to these elements like `element.addEventLisener(''focus'', onFocus)`\n- With `Ace` we may accomplish these patterns by creating `ref` variables or creating `ref` functions\n\n\n\n## `Ref` Variable\n- Helpful when we''d love to have an `element` as a `variable` \n\n  ```ts\n  import { onShowModal, Modal } from ''@ace/modal''\n\n  let inputRef: undefined | HTMLInputElement\n\n  onShowModal(''search'', () => {\n    inputRef?.focus()\n    inputRef?.select()\n  })\n\n  <Modal id="search">\n    <input ref={inputRef} />\n  </Modal>\n  ```\n\n\n\n## `Ref` Function\n- Called when an element `mounts`\n- Helpful when we''d love to bind listeners to an `element` w/in a `function`\n- The `types` we place on the function help us w/ `type-safety`\n    - Example: `return (element: HTMLInputElement | HTMLTextAreaElement) => {`\n    - Gives us `type errors` if we place this function on an element that does not `match` the `types`\n  ```ts\n  export function ExampleComponent (props: { name: string }) => {\n    return <canvas ref={refExample(props.name)} />\n  }\n\n  export function refExample(name: string) { // defining a ref function\n    return (canvas: HTMLCanvasElement) => {\n      console.log({ name, canvas }) // here we may attach listeners to canvas & access variables passed to refExample()\n    }\n  }\n  ```\n','\n',char(10)),2,1765532002000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(7,'Scope','scope',7,replace('## ScopeBE\n- Available @:\n    - `B4` Functions, example:\n        ```ts\n        export const sessionB4: B4<{ session: Session }> = async (scope) => {\n          scope.event.locals.session = await getSession(scope)\n        }\n        ```\n    - `ApiResolver` > `.res()` Functions, example:\n      ```ts\n      import { db } from ''@src/db/db''\n      import { vParser, vString } from ''@ace/vParser''\n      import { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\n      \n      \n      export const info = new ApiInfo({\n        method: ''GET'',\n        path: ''/api/post/:slug'',\n        parser: vParser.api({\n          pathParams: {\n            slug: vString()\n          }\n        })\n      })\n      \n      export const resolver = async (req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) => {\n        ''use server''\n      \n        return new ApiResolver(req)\n          .res(async (scope) => {\n            const post = await db.query.posts.findFirst({ where: (r, { eq }) => eq(r.slug, req.pathParams.slug) })\n            if(!post) throw new Error(''Please include a valid slug'', { cause: { slug: req.pathParams.slug } })\n            return scope.success(post)\n          })\n      }\n      \n      \n      export default createApi(''apiGetPost'', info, resolver)\n      ```\n- Features:\n    - `scope.respond()`\n        - Builds a `Response` based on the provided props\n        - `scope.success()`,  `scope.error()` & `scope.go()` each use `scope.respond()`\n        - The option to return a custom `Response` from an `API` or `B4` is available too btw\n        - Props:\n            ```ts\n            {\n              path?: T_Path, // redirect path string\n              data?: T_Data, // object\n              status?: number,\n              error?: AceError,\n              headers?: HeadersInit,\n              pathParams?: RoutePath2PathParams<T_Path>, // object (for redirect)\n              searchParams?: RoutePath2SearchParams<T_Path>, // object (for redirect)\n            }\n            ```\n    - `scope.success()`\n        - Creates a success `Response` w/ simple options\n        - For all options please call `scope.respond()`\n        - Props: `(data?: T_Data, status = 200)`\n    - `scope.error()`\n        - Creates a error `Response` w/ simple options\n        - For all options please call `scope.respond()`\n        - Props: `(message: string, status = 400)`\n    - `scope.go()`\n        - Creates a redirect `Response` w/ simple options\n        - For all options please call `scope.respond()`\n        - Props: `(path: T_Path, params?: { pathParams?: RoutePath2PathParams<T_Path>, searchParams?: RoutePath2SearchParams<T_Path> })`\n    - `scope.setCookie()`\n        - Set a cookie\n        - Props: `(name: string, value: string, options?: CookieSerializeOptions)`\n    - `scope.getCookie()`\n        - Get a cookie value by name\n        - Props: `(name: string)`\n    - `scope.clearCookie()`\n        - Delete a cookie by name\n        - Props: `(name: string)`\n    - `scope.liveEvent()`\n        - Helpful when you''d love to create an [Ace Live Server](#live-data-without-browser-refresh) `event`\n    - `scope.requestUrlOrigin`\n        - The origin of the current HTTP request URL\n\n\n\n## ScopeComponent\n- Available @:\n    - Any component via `import { scope } from ''@ace/scopeComponent''`\n    - `Layout > .component()`, example:\n        ```ts\n        import { Nav } from ''@src/Nav/Nav''\n        import { Layout } from ''@ace/layout''\n\n        export default new Layout()\n          .component(({ children }) => {\n            return <>\n              <Nav />\n              {children}\n            </>\n          })\n        ```\n    - `Route > .component()`, example:\n        ```ts\n        import { Route } from ''@ace/route''\n\n        export default new Route(''/'')\n          .component((scope) => {\n            return <>\n              <Show when={scope.bits.get(''apiExample'') === false} fallback={<Loading />}>\n                <Loaded />\n              </Show>\n            </>\n          })\n        ```\n    - `Route404 > .component()`, example:\n        ```ts\n        import { Route404 } from ''@ace/route404''\n\n        export default new Route404()\n          .component((scope) => {\n            return <>\n              <h1>{scope.location.pathname}</h1>\n            </>\n          })\n        ```\n- Features:\n    - `scope.pathParams`\n        - Path params as an object\n        - If using in a `createEffect()` THEN use `scope.PathParams()`\n    - `scope.PathParams()`\n        - Reactive path params that can be used in a `createEffect()`\n        - If not using in a `createEffect()` THEN use `scope.pathParams`\n    - `scope.searchParams`\n        - Search params as an object\n        - If using in a `createEffect()` THEN use `scope.SearchParams()`\n    - `scope.SearchParams()`\n        - Reactive search params that can be used in a `createEffect()`\n        - If not using in a `createEffect()` THEN use `scope.searchParams`\n    - `scope.location`\n        - Location as an object\n        - If using in a `createEffect()` THEN use `scope.Location()`\n    - `scope.Location()`\n        - Reactive location that can be used in a `createEffect()`\n        - IF not using in a `createEffect()` THEN use `scope.location`\n    - `scope.liveSubscribe()`\n        - Helpful when you''d love to create a ws connection to an [Ace Live Server](#live-data-without-browser-refresh)\n        - Example:\n            ```ts\n            onMount(() => {\n              const { ws, error } = scope.liveSubscribe({ stream: [''chatRoom'', id] })\n      \n              if (error?.message) showErrorToast(error.message)\n              else if (ws) {\n                ws.addEventListener(''message'', event => {\n                  console.log(event.data) // ✅ real-time data received!\n                })\n      \n                onCleanup(() => scope.liveUnsubscribe(ws))\n              }\n            })\n      \n            // ace.config.js ❤️ Prerequisite\n            export const config = {\n              liveHosts: {\n                local: ''localhost:8787'',\n              }\n            }\n            ```\n    - `scope.go()`\n        - Frontend redirect w/ simple options\n        - For all possible options please use `scope.Go()`\n        - Props:\n            ```ts\n            /**\n             * @param path - Redirect to this path, as defined @ new Route()\n             * @param params.pathParams - Path params\n             * @param params.searchParams - Search params\n             */\n            ```\n    - `scope.Go()`\n        - Frontend redirect w/ simple options\n        - For all possible options please use `scope.Go()`\n        - Props:\n            ```ts\n            /**\n             * @param path - Redirect to this path, as defined @ new Route()\n             * @param pathParams - Path params\n             * @param searchParams - Search params\n             * @param replace - Optional, defaults to false, when true this redirect will clear out a history stack entry \n             * @param scroll - Optional, defaults to true, if you''d like to scroll to the top of the page when done redirecting\n             * @param state - Optional, defaults to an empty object, must be an object that is serializable, available @ the other end via `fe.getLocation().state`\n             */\n            ```\n    - `scope.children`\n        - Get the children for a layout\n        - IF not a layout OR no children THEN `undefined`','\n',char(10)),2,1765532046000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(8,'Error Dictionary','error-dictionary',8,replace('# Error Dictionary\n- As we find common bugs 🐛 we''ll place their solutions here!\n\n\n\n## Standard Fix\n1. Stop all local servers running Ace\n1. Delete generated `.ace` folder\n1. IF you do not have any files placed into `routes` manually THEN delete `routes` folder\n1. `npm run dev`\n1. Ensure the port shown in `bash`, is the port in your `./ace.config.js`\n1. Open a new browser tab, place the url from `bash` into the browser & press `Enter`\n\n\n\n## Errors\n1. `bash: ace: command not found`\n    - The way `npm` works, is if we wanna go into a `bash` terminal & use a command like `ace` then the package that provides this command **must be globally installed**\n    - This is b/c bash can navigate to any directory, so all that is required is to globally install: `npm i @acets-team/ace -g`\n    - 🚨 This is not a requirment @ `package.json` > `scripts`, which is why `ace` commands  work here w/o a global install but is a standard `npm` requirement w/in a bash terminal ❤️\n1. `TypeError: Comp is not a function at createComponent`\n    - Ensure `app.tsx` has `import { createApp } from ''@ace/createApp''` and `export default createApp()`\n    - [Standard Fix](#standard-fix)\n1. `Error: <A> and ''use'' router primitives can be only used inside a Route.`\n    This is typically caused by something in the default export function @ `app.tsx` that should not be there, like `useParams()` or `useLocation()`. This function must only have items like  `<Router />`, `<FileRoutes />` or `<Route />`. Then w/in the component functions of the route or layout we may use router primatives\n    - [Standard Fix](#standard-fix)\n1. `Cannot read properties of null (reading ''push'') @ Effects.push.apply(Effects, e);` \n    - [Standard Fix](#standard-fix)\n1. `Error Unknown error @ solid-js/dist/dev.js`\n    - Fix any browser console errors\n    - [Standard Fix](#standard-fix)\n1. `Type [example] is not assignable to type ''IntrinsicAttributes & [example]. Property [example] does not exist on type ''IntrinsicAttributes & [example].ts(2322)`\n    - Ensure the props on your functional components are destructured so rather then `export function ExampleComponent(scope: ScopeComponent)` it should be `export function ExampleComponent({ scope }: { scope: ScopeComponent })` \n1. `Argument of type ''(scope: ScopeComponent<any, any>) => void'' is not assignable to parameter of type ''RouteComponent<any, any>''. Type ''void'' is not assignable to type ''Element''.`\n    - Change this:\n        ```ts\n        export default new Route(''/'')\n          .component((scope) => {\n            return scope.go(''/sign-in'')\n          })\n        ```\n    - To this:\n        ```ts\n        export default new Route(''/'')\n          .component((scope) => {\n            throw scope.go(''/sign-in'')\n          })\n        ```\n    - Throwing thankfully ends the inference loop of defining and returning a route 🙌\n','\n',char(10)),2,1765536195000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(9,'Ace Live Server','ace-live-server',2,replace('# Ace Live Server\n\n- Helpful when you''d love to show `real-time data` w/ no browser refresh required\n- Does not use `polling` (request every x seconds) b/c that is not real-time data\n- Does not use `standard web sockets` b/c they **charge when idle** (connected & waiting for an event)\n- Uses Cloudflare''s [hibernating websockets](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets) which are free when waiting for an event, but keep the connection open & allow 100,000 requests a month for free, cost example:\n\n| Amount | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| `3,000` | Concurrent Connections |\n| `10` | Events per Second |\n| `30` | Days in Month |\n| `86,400` | Seconds in Day |\n| `864,000` | Events per Day = Seconds in Day * Events per Second |\n| `$5` | [Paid Plan Cost](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing): B/c over 100,000 requests per day |\n| `25,920,000` | Events per Month = Events per Day * Days in Month |\n| `20` | Daily WS Reconnects |\n| `1,800,000` | WS Connects per Month = Concurrent Connections * Daily WS Reconnects * Days in Month |\n| `27,720,000` | Requests per Month = Events per Month + WS Connects per Month |\n| `10` | [Processing time per request (ms)](/docs/ace-live-server) |\n| `277,200,000` | Monthly processing time (ms) = Requests per Month * Processing time per request (ms) |\n| `277,200` | Monthly processing time (seconds) = Monthly processing time (ms) / 1,000 |\n| `0.128` | Memory allocated in GB |\n| `35,482` | Total GB/Second per Month |\n| `$0` | GB/Second Cost (free tier allows 400,000) |\n| `27.72` | Total Requests per Month (millions) |\n| `26.72` | Paid Requests per Month (millions) ([first million is free](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing)) = Total Requests per Month (millions) - 1 |\n| `$0.15` | [Cost per million requests](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing) |\n| `$4.01` | Requests Cost per month = Paid Requests per Month (millions) * Cost per million requests |\n| `$9.01` | Total Cost per Month |\n\n\n\n## How it works\n- App is running @ `localhost:3000` or `example.com`\n    - `Solid Start` app powered by `Vite`\n- Ace Live Server is running @ `localhost:3001` or `live.example.com`\n    - Cloudflare `Worker` + Cloudflare `Durable Object` powered by `wrangler`\n- App & Ace Live Server can share cookies between each other b/c they are on the same domain, just ensure the cookie is set like this\n  ```ts\n  import {env} from ''@ace/env''\n\n  scope.setCookie(sessionCookieName, jwt, {\n    maxAge: secWeek,\n    domain: env == ''prod''\n      ? ''.example.com'' // allows us to set cookies @ example.com & read cookie @ live.example.com\n      : undefined\n  })\n  ```\n- To create events App sends a request to the Ace Live Server\n    ```ts\n    // BE: Broadcast to thousands of concurrent connections\n    const res = await scope.liveEvent({\n      // events are grouped into streams\n      // streams can be a string or a tuple\n      stream: [''chatRoom'', id],\n      data: { aloha: true },\n    })\n    ```\n- To subscribe, subscribers establish hibernating websocket connections to Ace Live Server\n    ```ts\n    // FE: Real-time data w/ no browser refresh required\n    onMount(() => {\n      const { ws, error } = scope.liveSubscribe({ stream: [''chatRoom'', id] })\n\n      if (error?.message) showErrorToast(error.message)\n      else if (ws) {\n        ws.addEventListener(''message'', event => {\n          console.log(event.data) // ✅ real-time data received!\n        })\n\n        onCleanup(() => scope.liveUnsubscribe(ws))\n      }\n    })\n    ```\n\n\n\n## Example\n1. Prerequisites: Have an app running: \n    ```bash\n    npx create-ace-app@latest\n    ```\n1. Create an **Ace Live Server** (`Cloudflare Worker` + `Cloudflare Durable Object` that uses `hibernating web sockets`), bash:\n    ```bash\n    npx create-ace-live-server@latest\n    ```\n1. Start your `Ace Live Server` via `npm run dev`\n1. Now back @ your Website code:\n    1. Add `liveHosts` to `ace.config.js`\n        ```js\n        export const config = {\n          liveHosts: {\n            local: ''localhost:8787'',\n            prod: ''live.example.com''\n          }\n        }\n        ```\n    1. **Subscribe** to events w/ [ScopeComponent](/post/scope)\n    1. **Create Events** w/ [ScopeBE](#scope)\n1. 🚨 IF you would love to send messages from the browser `ws` THEN add an `onMessage` callback @ `createLiveDurableObject()`, example:\n    ```ts\n    import { createLiveWorker, createLiveDurableObject } from ''@ace/liveServer''\n\n\n    export default createLiveWorker() satisfies ExportedHandler<Env>\n\n\n    export const LiveDurableObject = createLiveDurableObject({\n      onMessage(props) {\n        console.log(''props'', props)\n      }\n    })\n    ```\n1. 🚨 & IF you would love to secure your `Ace Live Server`, example:\n    ```ts\n    import { jwtValidate } from ''@ace/jwtValidate''\n    import { createLiveWorker, createLiveDurableObject, readCookie } from ''@ace/liveServer''\n\n\n    export default createLiveWorker() satisfies ExportedHandler<Env>\n\n\n    export const LiveDurableObject = createLiveDurableObject({\n      onValidateEvent(request) { // IF valid THEN no return AND IF invalid THEN return Response\n        if (request.headers.get(''live_secret'') !== process.env.LIVE_SECRET) { // to create a password "ace password" in bash ❤️ & place this password in the .env of your app & the .env of your live server, so that only your app can call /event\n          return new Response(''Unauthorized'', { status: 400 })\n        }\n      },\n      async onValidateSubscribe(request) { // IF valid THEN no return AND IF invalid THEN return Response\n        const jwt = readCookie(request, ''aceJWT'')\n        const res = await jwtValidate({ jwt })\n        if (!res.isValid) return new Response(''Unauthorized'', { status: 400 })\n      }\n    })\n    ```\n1. To deploy your `Ace Live Server` w/ **git push**, follow our [deploy directions here](/post/deploy)!\n1. 🚨 To place `Ace Live Server` @ a subdomain so you may share cookies w/ your `App`, in your `wrangler.jsonc` set `routes` to: \n    ```json\n    {\n        "routes": [\n        {\n          "pattern": "live.example.com",\n          "zone_id": "123456789",\n          "custom_domain": true\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n    ```','\n',char(10)),3,1765538588000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(10,'Create Api''s','create-api',1,replace('# Create Api''s\n- Ace supports `GET`, `POST`, `PUT` & `DELETE`\n- Like `Hono`, on api request we determine what Api must be called, by efficiently [walking a tree](#build-time-ace)\n- Most meta-frameworks have moved toward `Server Functions`. While convenient, these are essentially `proprietary tunnels` b/c the framework generates a random path for each `server function`! 🤬 So this black box can''t be called from `curl`, a `python` script or a mobile `app` without significant reverse engineering\n- **Ace** provides the `type-safe` convenience of a server function while ensuring the path you set as seen below is your actual api path ❤️ So with **Ace** you can call your api''s from `curl`, a `python` script or a mobile `app`!\n\n\n\n## GET w/o Params\n  ```ts\n  import { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\n\n\n  export const info = new ApiInfo({\n    method: ''GET'',\n    path: ''/api/example''\n  })\n\n  export const resolver = async (req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) => {\n    ''use server'' // ❤️ this fn & it''s imports will not be in the FE build\n\n    return new ApiResolver(req)\n      .res(async (scope) => {\n        return scope.success(''aloha'') // calls scope.respond() for us which has the kitchen sink of options like requestInit (status, headers)\n      })\n  }\n\n  export default createApi(''apiExample'', info, resolver)\n  ```\n\n\n\n## GET w/ Params\n- In Ace an `ApiInfo.parser` is a function that validates a requests `params`, `body` & `headers`, throws if invalid and gives back parsed data if valid\n- Ace has 1st class [valibot](https://valibot.dev/guides/comparison/) support\n- Creating an `ApiInfo.parser` w/ `no/any` library is supported\n- The `ApiResolver.res()` function will only be called if the request passes the `ApiInfo.parser` validations\n- We may use these same validations on the [FE](/post/call-api) to ensure the Api is not called till validations pass\n- If the data is valid, `ApiResolver.res()` will includes type-safe & parsed params, example `scope.searchParams.limit` will be a type-safe `number` ❤️\n  ```ts\n  import { optional, vNum, vBool, vString, vParser } from ''@ace/vParser''\n  import { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\n\n\n  export const info = new ApiInfo({\n    method: ''GET'',\n    path: ''/api/example/:query/:potential?''\n    parser: vParser.api({\n      pathParams: {\n        query: vString(), potential: optional(vBool())\n      },\n      searchParams: {\n        limit: optional(vNum()), offset: optional(vNum())\n      }\n    })\n  })\n\n  export const resolver = async (req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) => {\n    ''use server''\n\n    return new ApiResolver(req)\n      .res(async (scope) => {\n        return scope.success({ // scope.[pathParams & searchParams] is type-safe!\n          pathParams: scope.pathParams,\n          searchParams: scope.searchParams,\n        })\n      })\n  }\n\n  export default createApi(''apiExample'', info, resolver)\n  ```\n\n\n\n## POST w/ JSON Body\n```ts\nimport { db } from ''@src/lib/db''\nimport { vParser, vString } from ''@ace/vParser''\nimport { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\n\nexport const info = new ApiInfo({\n  method: ''POST'',\n  path: ''/api/chat-message/:room'',\n  parser: vParser.api({\n    pathParams: { room: vNum() },\n    body: { chatMessage: vString() }\n  })\n})\n\nexport async function resolver(req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) {\n  ''use server''\n\n  return new ApiResolver(req)\n    .res(async (scope) => {\n      return scope.success( \n        db.saveChatMessage( // scope.[pathParams & body] is type-safe!\n          scope.pathParams.room,\n          scope.body.chatMessage\n        )\n      )\n    })\n}\n\nexport default createApi(''apiSaveChatMessage'', info, resolver)\n```\n\n\n\n## POST w/ FormData Body\n- `Default` to `FormData` until your uploads are over `15MB`\n- During an upload, `FormData` puts the `entire file` into `memory`. This is why you don''t wanna use `FormData` when the files size get very big to `save` your `memory`\n- Unlike `ReadableStream`''s, `FormData` let us send more then just the file in the body (as seen in the example below) which is very convenient\n  ```ts\n  import { bytesMB } from ''@ace/bytes''\n  import { vParser, vString, vImg } from ''@ace/vParser''\n  import { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\n\n\n  export const info = new ApiInfo({\n    method: ''POST'',\n    path: ''/api/form-data'',\n    parser: vParser.api({\n      formData: {\n        key: vString(),\n        img: vImg({ // ❤️ vFile() is available too!\n          maxSize: 3 * bytesMB,\n          allowedExtensions: [''svg'', ''png'', ''jpg'', ''webp''],\n        }),\n      }\n    })\n  })\n\n  export const resolver = async (req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) => {\n    ''use server''\n\n    return new ApiResolver(req)\n      .res(async (scope) => {\n        if (!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET) throw new Error(''!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET'')\n\n          const response = await scope.r2.put({ // ❤️ sending this file to Cloudflare R2! (10GB of free storage, no bandwidth costs, & this function can work if deployed to a cloudflare worker, node server, bun server etc.) \n            data: { key: scope.formData.key, file: scope.formData.img },\n            requestInit: {\n              headers: {\n                ...scope.requestHeaders,\n                ''ACE_R2_SECRET'': process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET\n              }\n            }\n          })\n\n        const {data, error} = await response.json()\n\n        if (error) return scope.error(error) \n        else return scope.success(data)\n      })\n  }\n\n  export default createApi(''apiFormData'', info, resolver)\n  ```\n\n\n\n## POST w/ ReadableStream\n- `ReadableStream`''s are best for `large` file uploads\n- A `ReadableStream` only looks at a snippet of the upload at a time and can upload `gigabytes` of data `safely` from a `memory` perspective\n- With `ReadableStream`''s the `entire body` is the `file`, so additional data must be in the `URL` (as seen in the example below)\n  ```ts\n  import { bytesMB } from ''@ace/bytes''\n  import { picklist, vNum, vParser, vString } from ''@ace/vParser''\n  import { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\n\n\n  export const info = new ApiInfo({\n    method: ''POST'',\n    readableStream: true, // 🚨 setting readableStream to true ensures scope.readableStream is defined below AND sets up your Stream Guard as described below\n    path: ''/api/readable-stream/:key'',\n    parser: vParser.api({\n      pathParams: { key: vString() },\n      headers: { // ❤️ validating headers w/ Ace!\n        ''content-type'': picklist([''image/png'', ''image/jpeg'', ''image/webp'', ''image/svg+xml'']),\n        ''content-length'': vNum({ range: { min: 1, max: 3 * bytesMB }, errorMessage: ''Please provide an image that is less then 3MB'' }),\n      }\n    })\n  })\n\n  export const resolver = async (req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) => {\n    ''use server''\n\n    return new ApiResolver(req)\n      .res(async (scope) => {\n        if (!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET) throw new Error(''!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET'')\n\n        const response = await scope.r2.put({\n          data: { key: scope.pathParams.key, file: scope.readableStream },\n          requestInit: {\n            headers: {\n              ...scope.requestHeaders,\n              ''ACE_R2_SECRET'': process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET\n            }\n          }\n        })\n\n        const {data, error} = await response.json()\n\n        if (error) return scope.error(error) \n        else return scope.success(data)\n      })\n  }\n\n  export default createApi(''apiReadableStream'', info, resolver)\n  ```\n\n\n\n## Stream Guard\n- Without a **Stream Guard**, a malicious actor could send a `Content-Length: 1MB header` but then keep the connection open and stream `100GB` of garbage data\n- This attack is called: `Denial of Service (DoS) via Resource Exhaustion`\n- IF `ApiInfo.readableStream` is set to `true` (as seen above) THEN **Ace** checks the bytes as they fly through the pipe & once the actual size exceeds the header size we `throw new Error(''Stream exceeded authorized limit'')`\n\n\n\n\n## @ Build Time Ace:\n- Generates 4 `tree''s` (one for each Api `method`) that helps us map a pathname to an Api!\n    - On request we split the request `pathname` into `segments` (ex: `[''api'', ''partners'']`), & then walk the `tree`\n    - The Api resolution speed is only dependent on `how many` pathname `segments` are in the url, tree example:\n      ```ts\n      export const treeGET = {\n        "static": {\n          "api": {\n            "static": {\n              "partners": {\n                "key": "apiGetPartners"\n              },\n              "post": {\n                "param": {\n                  "name": "slug",\n                  "node": {\n                    "key": "apiGetPost"\n                  }\n                }\n              },\n              "search": {\n                "param": {\n                  "name": "query",\n                  "node": {\n                    "key": "apiSearch"\n                  }\n                }\n              }\n            }\n          }\n        }\n      }\n      ```\n- Adds `[...api].ts` @ `src/routes/api/` to align api methods w/ the proper `tree`, example:\n  ```ts\n  import { callApi } from ''@ace/callApi''\n  import { treeGET } from ''@ace/treeGET''\n  import { treePUT } from ''@ace/treePUT''\n  import { treePOST } from ''@ace/treePOST''\n  import type { APIEvent } from ''@ace/types''\n  import { treeDELETE } from ''@ace/treeDELETE''\n  \n  \n  export async function GET(event: APIEvent) {\n    ''use server''\n    return callApi(event, treeGET)\n  }\n  \n  \n  export async function POST(event: APIEvent) {\n    ''use server''\n    return callApi(event, treePOST)\n  }\n  \n  \n  export async function PUT(event: APIEvent) {\n    ''use server''\n    return callApi(event, treePUT)\n  }\n  \n  \n  export async function DELETE(event: APIEvent) {\n    ''use server''\n    return callApi(event, treeDELETE)\n  }\n  ```\n- Generates a map to point Api `names` from the `tree''s` to their proper `modules`. This map gives us:\n    - `Type-safety`, b/c the `request` and `response` types come from these `modules` \n    - A `build url function`, so that when we build url''s, there is `no regex required` & it''s as fast as possible. Map example:\n      ```ts\n      import { buildUrl } from ''./buildUrl''\n      import type { MapBuildUrlProps } from ''./types''\n\n\n      export const mapApis = {\n        ''apiGetPartners'': {\n          api: () => import("../../src/api/apiGetPartners").then((m) => m.default),\n          info: () => import("../../src/api/apiGetPartners").then((m) => m.info),\n          resolver: () => import("../../src/api/apiGetPartners").then((m) => m.resolver),\n          buildUrl: (props?: MapBuildUrlProps) => buildUrl({ ...props, segments: [["api"],["partners"]] }),\n        },\n        ''apiGetPost'': {\n          api: () => import("../../src/api/apiGetPost").then((m) => m.default),\n          info: () => import("../../src/api/apiGetPost").then((m) => m.info),\n          resolver: () => import("../../src/api/apiGetPost").then((m) => m.resolver),\n          buildUrl: (props?: MapBuildUrlProps) => buildUrl({ ...props, segments: [["api"],["post"],["slug","r"]] }),\n        },\n        ''apiSearch'': {\n          api: () => import("../../src/api/apiSearch").then((m) => m.default),\n          info: () => import("../../src/api/apiSearch").then((m) => m.info),\n          resolver: () => import("../../src/api/apiSearch").then((m) => m.resolver),\n          buildUrl: (props?: MapBuildUrlProps) => buildUrl({ ...props, segments: [["api"],["search"],["query","r"]] }),\n        },\n      } as const\n      ```\n','\n',char(10)),4,1765613396000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(11,'Call Api''s','call-api',2,replace('# Call Api''s\n- Ace provides **3 classes** to help us call our Api''s w/ **type-safe** `request` & `response` **autocomplete**!\n- The `Load` & `Stream` classes are optimal when Api data should be fetched **DURING** the page load\n- The `Async` class is optimal when Api data should be fetched **AFTER** the page load\n\n\n\n## Load\n- The `Load` class is optimal when Api data **must** be indexed by `search engines`\n- Syncs the `Api` Load w/ the `Page` Load\n- 🚨 IF this Api may set cookies use `Load` & not `Stream`, IF this Api may redirect to another Api that may set cookies either is optimal\n- Search engine web crawlers will `always` see this data\n- IF variables w/in `req` change (ex: move from one page to the next) THEN `Load` is called again w/ the new request data & the `ui` is updated!\n- `setHeadings` gives us the headings from w/in the markdown so that we can have an `On this Page` component\n- The page `<Title />` & `<AceMarkdown />` will always be available to search engines!\n  ```ts\n  function StreamExample() {\n    const baseAtom = useAtom()\n\n    const post = new Load({\n      fn: apiGetPost,\n      queryKey: ''apiGetPost'',\n      runOnWindowToggle: true, // Optional, defaults to `false`, IF `true` AND someone returns to tab THEN `post.run()` is called\n      runOnNetworkToggle: true, // Optional, defaults to `false`, IF `true` AND someone regains wifi THEN `post.run()` is called\n      req: () => ({ pathParams: scope.PathParams() }), // ❤️ may also pass body, searchParams & requestInit here\n    })\n\n    return <>\n      <post.ui suspense={d => <>\n        <Title>📝 Post · {d?.title}</Title>\n\n        <AceMarkdown\n          content={d?.content}\n          setHeadings={(v) => baseAtom.set(''headings'', v)}\n      </>} /> \n    </>\n  }\n  ```\n\n\n\n## Stream\n- The `Stream` class is optimal when fast `First Contentful Paint` scores from `Google Lighthouse` is the priority, b/c static content is rendered immediately\n- When the Api is `loading` BUT the rest of the page is `ready`:\n    1. Response is provided w/ static content & `onLoad` components (default `onLoad` is a `css` spinner)\n    1. [`Solid''s <Suspense/>`](https://docs.solidjs.com/reference/components/suspense) creates childen **in memory**\n    1. Api data is `Streamed` to the browser\n    1. Solid adds children to the page w/ Api data `included`\n- By setting a `store` prop we sync the api response w/ a `store` so that on refresh we will immediately show data from the `store` & once the updated data is available it''ll display\n- 🚨 IF this Api may set cookies use `Load` & not `Stream`, IF this Api may redirect to another Api that may set cookies either is optimal\n- The `for` prop automatically places our template w/in [`Solid''s <Suspense/>`](https://docs.solidjs.com/reference/components/suspense) \n  ```ts\n  function LoadExample() {\n    const baseAtom = useAtom()\n\n    const partners = new Stream({\n      fn: apiGetPartners,\n      queryKey: ''partners'',\n      atom: [baseAtom, ''partners''],\n      runOnWindowToggle: true, // Optional, defaults to `false`, IF `true` AND someone returns to tab THEN `partners.run()` is called\n      runOnNetworkToggle: true, // Optional, defaults to `false`, IF `true` AND someone regains wifi THEN `partners.run()` is called\n    })\n\n    return <>\n      <partners.ui for={d => <h1>{d?.company}</h1>} />\n    </>\n  }\n  ```\n\n\n\n## Async w/ JSON\n- If you''d love to get Api data during page load please use `Load` or `Stream`\n- If you''d love to query an Api function off page load (ex: onClick) please use `Async`\n- `Async` let''s us pass a desired `req` (request data to be sent to the Api) when we call `run()`\n- The creation of the Async instance is not an async happening, but calling `run()` is so that will need to be in an async function\n- `createOnSubmit()` provides the event which can give us access to the `<form>` that was submitted\n  ```ts\n  import { Async } from ''@ace/async''\n  import { vApiInfo } from ''@ace/vParser''\n  import { mergeArrays } from ''@ace/merge''\n  import { createOnSubmit } from ''@ace/createOnSubmit''\n  import apiSaveChatMessage, { info } from ''@src/api/apiSaveChatMessage''\n\n\n  function AsyncExample() {\n    const {sync, store} = useAtom()\n\n    const save = new Async(apiSaveChatMessage)\n\n    const onSubmit = createOnSubmit(async ({ event }) => {\n      const body = vApiInfo.body(info.parser, { chatMessage: store.chatMessage })\n\n      const res = await save.run({ body })\n\n      if (res.data) {\n        event.target.reset() // reset form\n        sync(''chatMessages'', mergeArrays(store.chatMessages, res.data))\n      }\n    })\n\n    return <>\n      <form onSubmit={onSubmit} ref={refFormReset()}>\n        <div class="top">\n          <Refresh onClick={onRefreshClick} tooltipContent="Refresh Chat" />\n          <input ref={refBind(''chatMessage'')} name="chatMessage" type="text" placeholder="Send a message..." autocomplete="off" />\n          <button type="submit" aria-label="Send chat message">{svg_up()}</button>\n        </div>\n        <Messages name="chatMessage" />\n      </form>\n    </>\n  }\n  ```\n\n\n\n## Async w/ Debounce\n- `Async` can be called w/in an async function w/ whatever `req` (request data) we wanna send it that aligns w/ the `apiSearch` types\n- `refBind` gives us 2 way data binding between our store & this input, so b/c the `searchQuery Atom` saves to index db, on refresh the search query remains\n  ```ts\n  import { debounce } from ''@ace/debounce''\n  import { showErrorToast } from ''@ace/toast''\n\n\n  function DebounceExample() {\n    const baseAtom = useAtom()\n\n    const search = new Async(apiSearch)\n\n    const onSearch = debounce(async (query: string) => {\n      if (!query) baseAtom.set(''searchResults'', [])\n      else {\n        const res = await search.run({ pathParams: { query } })\n\n        if (res.error?.message) showErrorToast(res.error.message)\n        else if (res.data) baseAtom.set(''searchResults'', res.data)\n      }\n    }, 180)\n\n    return <>\n      <input\n        onInput={(e) => onSearch(e.currentTarget.value)}\n        ref={baseAtom.refBind(''searchQuery'')}\n        type="text" name="search" autocomplete="off" />\n    </>\n  }\n  ```\n\n\n\n## Async w/ FormData\n- `createOnSubmit()` provides an `fd()` function to get access to form values by name and the `formData` object to send to the Api\n  ```ts\n  import { Async } from ''@ace/async''\n  import { Submit } from ''@ace/submit''\n  import { vApiInfo } from ''@ace/vParser''\n  import { Messages } from ''@ace/messages''\n  import { createOnSubmit } from ''@ace/createOnSubmit''\n  import { showToast, showErrorToast } from ''@ace/toast''\n  import apiFormData, { info } from ''@src/api/apiFormData''\n\n\n  function FormDataExample() {\n    const useApiFormData = new Async(apiFormData)\n\n    const onSubmit = createOnSubmit(async ({ fd }) => {\n      const img = fd(''img'') // gets value by <input /> name\n\n      const formData = vApiInfo.formData(info.parser, { // using the ApiInfo.parser defined above\n        img,\n        key: img instanceof File ? img.name : undefined,\n      })\n\n      const res = await useApiFormData.run({ formData })\n\n      if (res.error?.message) showErrorToast(res.error.message)\n      else showToast({ value: ''Uploaded!'', type: ''success'' })\n    })\n\n    return <>\n      <form onSubmit={onSubmit}>\n        <input name="img" type="file" />\n        <Messages name="img" /> {/* img errors provided here */}\n        <Submit hook={useApiFormData} label="Save" $button={{ class: ''brand'' }} /> {/* Shows a loading spinner when useApiFormData has a status of ''loading'' */}\n      </form>\n    </>\n  }\n  ```\n','\n',char(10)),4,1765616772000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(12,'File Uploads','file-uploads',1,replace('# File Uploads\n- Ace can be deployed `anywhere` Node can be deployed\n- If you have `your own server` you can upload files on your server but you''ll need to pay `bandwidth fee''s`\n- Ace has first class support for [Cloudflare R2](https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/pricing/), so that `no matter where your server is deployed` (Cloudflare, AWS, Bun) you can spin up a Cloudflare R2 Worker and upload files to it\n- An `R2 Worker` is just a `serverless endpoint` that let''s us `read` and `write` to `Cloudflare R2` Storage and allows [100,000 requests](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/) a day for free\n- If you `don''t wanna use R2`, just follow the directions to when you have the file on your server and then do whatever you want with the files from there but we highly recommend R2 b/c you get `10GB` of free storage, every GB after that is `1.5` USD cents and `no bandwidth fees`!\n\n\n\n## FormData\n- `Default` to `FormData` until your uploads are over `15MB`\n- During an upload, `FormData` puts the `entire file` into `memory`. This is why you don''t wanna use `FormData` when the files size get very big to `save` your `memory`\n- Unlike `ReadableStream`''s, `FormData` let us send more then just the file in the body (as seen in the example below) which is very convenient\n\n\n\n## Api FormData\n```ts\nimport { bytesMB } from ''@ace/bytes''\nimport { vParser, vString, vImg } from ''@ace/vParser''\nimport { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\n\n\nexport const info = new ApiInfo({\n  method: ''POST'',\n  path: ''/api/form-data'',\n  parser: vParser.api({\n    formData: {\n      key: vString(),\n      img: vImg({ // vFile() is also available\n        maxSize: 3 * bytesMB,\n        allowedExtensions: [''svg'', ''png'', ''jpg'', ''webp''],\n      }),\n    }\n  })\n})\n\nexport const resolver = async (req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) => {\n  ''use server''\n\n  return new ApiResolver(req)\n    .res(async (scope) => {\n      if (!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET) throw new Error(''!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET'') // this secret will be in the .env file of our Cloudflare R2 worker too\n\n        const response = await scope.r2.put({\n        data: {\n          key: scope.formData.key, // ts knows this is a string\n          file: scope.formData.img, // ts knows this is a File\n        },\n        requestInit: {\n          headers: {\n            ...scope.requestHeaders, // this passes headers like content-type & content-length to our r2 worker\n            ''ACE_R2_SECRET'': process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET // also saved in the .env file of the r2 worker\n          }\n        }\n      })\n\n      const {data, error} = await response.json()\n\n      if (error) return scope.error(error) \n      else return scope.success(data)\n    })\n}\n\nexport default createApi(''apiFormData'', info, resolver)\n```\n\n\n\n## Component FormData\n```ts\nimport { Async } from ''@ace/async''\nimport { Submit } from ''@ace/submit''\nimport { vApiInfo } from ''@ace/vParser''\nimport { Messages } from ''@ace/messages''\nimport { createOnSubmit } from ''@ace/createOnSubmit''\nimport { showToast, showErrorToast } from ''@ace/toast''\nimport apiFormData, { info } from ''@src/api/apiFormData''\n\nfunction FormDataUpload() {\n  const useApiFormData = new Async(apiFormData)\n\n  const onSubmit = createOnSubmit(async ({ fd }) => {\n    const img = fd(''img'') // gets value by <input /> name\n\n    const formData = vApiInfo.formData(info.parser, { // using the ApiInfo.parser defined above\n      img,\n      key: img instanceof File ? img.name : undefined,\n    })\n\n    const res = await useApiFormData.run({ formData })\n\n    if (res.error?.message) showErrorToast(res.error.message)\n    else showToast({ value: ''Uploaded!'', type: ''success'' })\n  })\n\n  return <>\n    <form onSubmit={onSubmit}>\n      <input name="img" type="file" />\n      <Messages name="img" /> {/* img errors provided here */}\n      <Submit hook={useApiFormData} label="Save" $button={{ class: ''brand'' }} /> {/* Shows a loading spinner when useApiFormData has a status of ''loading'' */}\n    </form>\n  </>\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## ReadableStream\n- `ReadableStream`''s are best for `large` file uploads\n- A `ReadableStream` only looks at a snippet of the upload at a time and can upload `gigabytes` of data `safely` from a `memory` perspective\n- With `ReadableStream`''s the `entire body` is the `file`, so additional data must be in the `URL` (as seen in the example below)\n\n\n\n## Api ReadableStream\n```ts\nimport { bytesMB } from ''@ace/bytes''\nimport { picklist, vNum, vParser, vString } from ''@ace/vParser''\nimport { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\n\n\nexport const info = new ApiInfo({\n  method: ''POST'',\n  readableStream: true, // 🚨 setting readableStream to true ensures scope.readableStream is defined below AND sets up your Stream Guard as described below\n  path: ''/api/readable-stream/:key'', // for ReadableStream, the entire body is the file, so additional data must be in the URL, which is a perk of FormData\n  parser: vParser.api({\n    pathParams: { key: vString() },\n    headers: { // ❤️ validating headers w/ Ace!\n      ''content-type'': picklist([''image/png'', ''image/jpeg'', ''image/webp'', ''image/svg+xml'']),\n      ''content-length'': vNum({ range: { min: 1, max: 3 * bytesMB }, errorMessage: ''Please provide an image that is less then 3MB'' }),\n    }\n  })\n})\n\nexport const resolver = async (req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) => {\n  ''use server''\n\n  return new ApiResolver(req)\n    .res(async (scope) => {\n      if (!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET) throw new Error(''!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET'')\n\n      const response = await scope.r2.put({\n        data: { key: scope.pathParams.key, file: scope.readableStream },\n        requestInit: {\n          headers: {\n            ...scope.requestHeaders,\n            ''ACE_R2_SECRET'': process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET\n          }\n        }\n      })\n\n      const {data, error} = await response.json()\n\n      if (error) return scope.error(error) \n      else return scope.success(data)\n    })\n}\n\nexport default createApi(''apiReadableStream'', info, resolver)\n```\n\n\n\n## Stream Guard\n- Without a **Stream Guard**, a malicious actor could send a `Content-Length: 1MB header` but then keep the connection open and stream `100GB` of garbage data\n- This attack is called: `Denial of Service (DoS) via Resource Exhaustion`\n- IF `ApiInfo.readableStream` is set to `true` THEN **Ace** checks the bytes as they fly through the pipe & once the actual size exceeds the header size we `throw new Error(''Stream exceeded authorized limit'')`\n\n\n\n## Component ReadableStream\n```ts\nfunction ReadableStreamUpload() {\n  const scope = useScope()\n  const useApiReadableStream = new Async(apiReadableStream)\n\n  const onSubmit = createOnSubmit(async ({ fd }) => {\n    const file = fd(''picture'')\n    if (!(file instanceof File)) return scope.messages.set({ name: ''picture'', value: ''Please upload a picture'' })\n\n    const res = await useApiReadableStream.run({ file, pathParams: { key: file.name } })\n\n    if (res.error?.message) showErrorToast(res.error.message)\n    else showToast({ value: ''Uploaded!'', type: ''success'' })\n  })\n\n  return <>\n    <form onSubmit={onSubmit}>\n      <input name="picture" type="file" />\n\n      {/* On ApiInfo validation, anyone of these ApiInfo parser keys may have an error */}\n      {/* B/c they all apply to this input, we place these <Messages/> components by this input */}\n      <Messages name="picture" />\n      <Messages name="headers.content-type" />\n      <Messages name="headers.content-length" />\n\n      {/* Will show a loading spinner while useApiReadableStream has a status of ''loading'' */}\n      <Submit hook={useApiReadableStream} label="Save" $button={{ class: ''brand'' }} />\n    </form>\n  </>\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## R2 Worker\n- A `Cloudflare R2 Worker`:\n    - Is a collection of `serverless` Api routes, that upload `files` to `R2`\n    - Can be called from anywhere (Node, another Worker, Bun, Bash, etc)\n- We recommend this flow:\n    1. Validate file in `browser`\n    1. Validate file in `application server`\n    1. Send file to `R2 Worker` to be uploaded to R2\n- 🚨 Security:\n    - Create a secret `ace password` THEN save it in the `.env` file of your application server and THEN save it in the `.env` file of the R2 Worker and THEN send this secret in the header\n    - `Reject` requests in the R2 Worker that `don''t` have this `header`, example:\n- Setup:\n    1. Bash:\n        ```bash\n        npx create-ace-r2@latest\n        ```\n    1. Enable R2 through the Cloudflare Dashboard\n        1. Quick Search -> `R2`\n        1. Add PayPal or Credit Card Information \n    1. Create R2 Bucket in Cloud, bash:\n        ```bash\n        npx wrangler r2 bucket create <YOUR_BUCKET_NAME>\n        ```\n    1. To access your new R2 Bucket in your Worker, add the following snippet to your configuration file:\n        ```json\n        {\n            "r2_buckets": [\n                {\n                    "bucket_name": "<YOUR_BUCKET_NAME>",\n                    "binding": "<YOUR_BINDING_NAME>"\n                }\n            ]\n        }\n        ```\n    1. Verify that the bucket was created:\n        - `npx wrangler r2 bucket list`\n    1. Update types w/ new binding:\n        - `npm run cf-typegen`\n    1. Create a secret `ace password` (w/ WebCrypto Api, creates a random sequence of `64 random bytes`, encodes it into a `URL-safe Base64 string` & is generally considered extremely resistant to brute-force and birthday attacks)\n        - To run an `ace` command in a bash terminal we must globally install (npm requirement) so: `npm i @acets-team/ace -g`\n    1. Save secret in the `.env` file of your application server\n    1. Save secret in the `.env` file of the R2 Worker\n    1. Create Worker, `index.ts`:\n        ```ts\n        import { r2Worker } from ''@ace/r2Worker''\n\n\n        // there is also an onValidateGetOne() if you''d like to do validations for when a get for one object is done\n        export default r2Worker({\n          binding: ''acets'',\n          onValidatePut: ensureAuthenticated,\n          onValidateDelete: ensureAuthenticated,\n          onValidateGetList: ensureAuthenticated,\n        })\n\n\n        function ensureAuthenticated(request: Request) {\n          if (request.headers.get(''ACE_R2_SECRET'') !== process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET) {\n            return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: ''Unauthorized'' }), {\n              status: 400,\n              headers: { ''content-type'': ''application/json'' }\n            })\n          }\n        }\n        ```\n    1. Test r2 worker @ `http://localhost:8787`\n        - This html page only workers when then `env` is local\n        - You''ll need to comment the onValidatePut, onValidateDelete and onValidateGetList to get the local test to work b/c the local html file does not know the secret\n        - ![Ace R2 HTML Test Page](https://i.imgur.com/WYrdMMw.jpeg)\n    1. You automatically share cookies between your application server and r2 worker on localhost, but in production if you''d love to do this to authenticate jwt tokens for example w/in the r2 worker you need both the application and the r2 worker to be on the same domain, this is super easy w/ cloudflare, just add to your `wrangler.jsonc`:\n        ```json\n        {\n            "routes": [\n            {\n              "pattern": "r2.example.com",\n              "zone_id": "123456789",\n              "custom_domain": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n        ```\n\n\n\n## Ace Config\n- To get `scope.r2` to work, add to your `ace.config.js` file, the origin to your r2 worker, example:\n  ```ts\n  {\n    r2Origins: {\n      local: ''http://localhost:8787''\n      prod: ''https://r2.example.com''\n    }\n  }\n  ```\n\n\n\n## R2 URL\n```ts\nimport { r2Url } from ''@ace/r2Url''\n\n<img src={r2Url({ key: ''example'' })} />\n```\n\n\n\n## Api Remove\n```ts\nimport { vParser, vString } from ''@ace/vParser''\nimport { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\n\n\nexport const info = new ApiInfo({\n  method: ''POST'',\n  path: ''/api/remove-img/:key'',\n  parser: vParser.api({\n    pathParams: { key: vString() }\n  })\n})\n\nexport const resolver = async (req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) => {\n  ''use server''\n\n  return new ApiResolver(req)\n    .res(async (scope) => {\n      if (!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET) throw new Error(''!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET'')\n\n      const response = await scope.r2.delete({\n        key: scope.pathParams.key,\n        requestInit: {\n          headers: { ''ACE_R2_SECRET'': process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET }\n        }\n      })\n\n      const {data, error} = await response.json()\n\n      if (error) return scope.error(error) \n      else return scope.success(data)\n    })\n}\n\nexport default createApi(''apiRemoveImg'', info, resolver)\n```\n\n\n\n## Component Remove\n```ts\nfunction RemoveExample() {\n  const useApiRemoveImg = new Async(apiRemoveImg)\n\n  const onRemoveImg = createOnSubmit(async ({ fd }) => {\n    const pathParams = vApiInfo.pathParams(apiRemoveImgInfo.parser, {\n      key: fd(''key'')\n    })\n\n    const res = await useApiRemoveImg.run({ pathParams })\n\n    if (res.error?.message) showErrorToast(res.error.message)\n    else showToast({ value: ''Removed!'', type: ''success'' })\n  })\n\n  return <>\n    <form onSubmit={onRemoveImg}>\n      <input name="key" type="text" placeholder="Remove Key" />\n      <Messages name="key" />\n      <Submit hook={useApiRemoveImg} label="Save" $button={{ class: ''brand'' }} />\n    </form>\n  </>\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## Api List\n```ts\nimport type { R2ResList, R2ResEither } from ''@ace/vanilla''\nimport { createApi, ApiInfo, ApiResolver, ApiInfo2Req } from ''@ace/api''\nimport { optional, vNum, vParser, vString, vSearchParamsArray } from ''@ace/vParser''\n\n\nexport const info = new ApiInfo({\n  method: ''GET'',\n  path: ''/api/list'',\n  parser: vParser.api({\n    searchParams: {\n      prefix: optional(vString()),\n      cursor: optional(vString()),\n      delimiter: optional(vString()),\n      startAfter: optional(vString()),\n      limit: optional(vNum({ allowNegative: false })),\n      include: optional(vSearchParamsArray([''httpMetadata'', ''customMetadata'']))\n    }\n  })\n})\n\nexport const resolver = async (req: ApiInfo2Req<typeof info>) => {\n  ''use server''\n\n  return new ApiResolver(req)\n    .res(async (scope) => {\n      if (!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET) throw new Error(''!process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET'')\n\n      const response = await scope.r2.list({\n        options: scope.searchParams, // ❤️ already the type options requires!\n        requestInit: {\n          headers: { ''ACE_R2_SECRET'': process.env.ACE_R2_SECRET }\n        }\n      })\n\n      const { data, error } = await response.json() as R2ResEither\n\n      if (error) return scope.error(error) \n      else return scope.success(data as R2ResList)\n    })\n}\n\nexport default createApi(''apiGetList'', info, resolver)\n```\n\n\n\n## Component List\n```ts\nimport { Async } from ''@ace/async''\nimport { Submit } from ''@ace/submit''\nimport { vApiInfo } from ''@ace/vParser''\nimport { dateRead } from ''@ace/dateRead''\nimport { createSignal, For } from ''solid-js''\nimport type { Async2ResData } from ''@ace/types''\nimport { createOnSubmit } from ''@ace/createOnSubmit''\nimport { showToast, showErrorToast } from ''@ace/toast''\nimport apiGetList, { info as apiGetListInfo } from ''@src/api/apiGetList''\n\n\nfunction ListExample() {\n  const useApiListR2 = new Async(apiGetList)\n\n  const [list, setList] = createSignal<Async2ResData<typeof useApiListR2>>() // will hold our response data\n\n  const onSubmit = createOnSubmit(async ({ fd }) => {\n    const searchParams = vApiInfo.searchParams(apiGetListInfo.parser, {\n      limit: fd(''limit'') || undefined, // ❤️ fd() handles checkbox to array for us\n      prefix: fd(''prefix'') || undefined,\n      cursor: fd(''cursor'') || undefined,\n      include: fd(''include'') || undefined,\n      delimiter: fd(''delimiter'') || undefined,\n      startAfter: fd(''startAfter'') || undefined\n    })\n\n    const res = await useApiListR2.run({ searchParams })\n\n    if (res.error?.message) showErrorToast(res.error.message)\n    else if (res.data) {\n      setList(res.data)\n      showToast({ value: ''Success!'', type: ''success'' })\n    }\n  })\n\n  return <>\n    <form onSubmit={onSubmit}>\n      <div>\n        <input name="limit" type="number" placeholder="Limit" />\n        <Messages name="limit" />\n      </div>\n\n      <div>\n        <input name="prefix" type="text" placeholder="Prefix" />\n        <Messages name="prefix" />\n      </div>\n\n      <div>\n        <input name="cursor" type="text" placeholder="Cursor" />\n        <Messages name="cursor" />\n      </div>\n\n      <div>\n        <input name="delimiter" type="text" placeholder="Delimiter" />\n        <Messages name="delimiter" />\n      </div>\n\n      <div>\n        <input name="startAfter" type="text" placeholder="Start After" />\n        <Messages name="startAfter" />\n      </div>\n\n      <fieldset>\n        <legend>Include:</legend>\n\n        <div>\n          <input type="checkbox" id="httpMetadata" name="include" value="httpMetadata" checked />\n          <label for="httpMetadata">Http Metadata</label>\n        </div>\n\n        <div>\n          <input type="checkbox" id="customMetadata" name="include" value="customMetadata" />\n          <label for="customMetadata">Custom Metadata</label>\n        </div>\n      </fieldset>\n      <Messages name="include" />\n      \n      <Submit hook={useApiListR2} label="Get" $button={{ class: ''brand'' }} />\n    </form>\n\n    <For each={list()}>{\n      (item) => <>\n        <div>\n          <h1>key: {item.key}</h1>\n          <div>etag: {item.etag}</div>\n          <div>httpEtag: {item.httpEtag}</div>\n          <div>uploaded: {dateRead({ date: item.uploaded })}</div>\n          <div>httpMetadata: <code>{JSON.stringify(item.httpMetadata)}</code></div>\n          <div>customMetadata: <code>{JSON.stringify(item.customMetadata)}</code></div>\n        </div>\n      </>\n    }</For>\n  </>\n}\n```\n','\n',char(10)),3,1766452462000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(13,'VsCode Tips','vscode-tips',6,replace('# VS Code Tips\n- These tips apply to `VsCode` or `any VsCode derivative` like [VsCodium](https://vscodium.com/)!\n\n\n## Tips\n1. How to show `intellisense` (typescipt autocomplete) dropdown?\n    - `Control` + `Space`\n    - ![](https://i.imgur.com/bwDbEMA.png)\n\n1. How to reload?\n    - `Command` + `Shift` + `P`\n    - Type: `reload window`\n    - Press `Enter`\n\n1. How to open `settings.json`\n    - `Command` + `Shift` + `P`\n    - Type: `user settings json`\n    - Press `Enter`\n\n1. How to get VS Code to create `<div class="example"></div>` on `.example`\n    - @ VS Code `settings.json` add:\n        ```json\n        {\n          "emmet.includeLanguages": {\n            "typescriptreact": "html",\n            "javascriptreact": "html"\n          }\n        }\n        ```\n    - Reload VS Code!\n\n1. How to run `code .` in `bash` & have it open a new instance in that directory\n    - `Command` + `Shift` + `P`\n    - Type: `Shell Command: Install ''code'' command in PATH`\n    - Press `Enter`\n\n1. How to alter icon for `.tsx` files\n    - Download the `Symbols` extension by `Miguel Solorio`\n    - Bash cd into `~/.vscode/extensions/`\n    - Bash cd `miguelsolorio.symbols-` w/ the proper version\n    - Bash: `code .`\n    - @ `/src/icons/files/` place image\n    - @ `/src/symbol-icon-theme.json` w/in `iconDefinitions` place `"ace": { "iconPath": "./icons/files/[ image name & extension ]" },`\n    - @ `fileExtensions` update `"tsx": "ace",` & anywhere else ya love!\n    - @ VS Code `settings.json` add:\n        ```js\n        "symbols.files.associations": {\n          "*.jsx": "ace",\n          "*.tsx": "ace"\n        }\n        ```\n    - Reload VS Code!\n','\n',char(10)),1,1766628480000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(14,'B4 (Middleware)','b4',3,replace('# B4 (Middleware)\n- Ace middleware is different then `Express` middleware and for this reason we use a different name. We call this idea `b4 (before) functions` b/c they run **before the `ApiResolver.res()` function** does\n- IF a `b4` functions returns a `Response` OR `throws` an `Error` THEN:\n    - That `Response` is provided\n    - No remaining `b4` functions are called\n    - `ApiResolver.res()` is not called\n- 🚨 To `persist` data to downstream b4 functions or to the downstream `ApiResolver.res()`, place data into `scope.event.locals` and update the functions generic type. This lets downstream functions know the type of your persisted data\n- A `b4` function runs in the same context as your `ApiResolver.res()` does, so there is no need to not call your database w/in one ❤️\n- `b4` functions run one at a time in the order they are defined in your array. So in this example, `sessionB4` runs first and then `adminB4` runs next:\n- `b4` functions are given `ScopeBE`, aka the same `scope` that is given to `ApiResolver.res()`\n![Ace B4 (Middleware)](https://i.imgur.com/fXKI6bN.png)\n\n\n\n## Auth Example\n```ts\nimport type { B4 } from ''@ace/types''\nimport { getSession } from ''./getSession''\nimport type { Session } from ''@src/lib/types''\n\n\nexport const sessionB4: B4<{ session: Session }> = async (scope) => {\n  scope.event.locals.session = await getSession(scope)\n}\n\nexport const adminB4: B4<{ session: Session }> = async (scope) => {\n  if (!scope.event.locals.session.isAdmin) {\n    return scope.error(''Unauthorized'', 403)\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## Rate Limit Example\n```ts\nimport type { B4 } from ''@ace/types''\nimport { isOverRateLimit } from ''./isOverRateLimit''\n\nexport const rateLimitB4: B4 = async (scope) => {\n  if (await isOverRateLimit(scope.event.clientAddress)) {\n    return scope.error(''Too many requests'', 429)\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n## CORS Example\n```ts\nimport type { B4 } from ''@ace/types''\nimport { configOrigins } from ''@ace/env''\n\nexport const corsB4: B4 = async (scope) => {\n  const origin = scope.requestHeaders[''origin'']\n\n  if (!origin || !configOrigins.has(origin)) {\n    return scope.error(''Security Violation: Unauthorized Origin'', 403)\n  }\n}\n```\n','\n',char(10)),4,1766637488000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(15,'Layouts','layouts',4,replace('# Layouts\n- In the `ace.config.js` we specify an `app` directory\n- During `ace build <env>`, Ace will search the `app` directory for any layouts\n- With Ace there are 2 types of layouts. The `RootLayout` & `SubLayouts`\n\n\n\n## RootLayout\n- All Routes will be placed within this `RootLayout`\n- ❤️ If an Api request is made w/in the `RootLayout`, this data is `NOT` re-requested as we navigate between pages but is `shared` as seen in the example below, which is the `RootLayout` for this site:\n  ```ts\n  import { RootLayout } from ''@ace/rootLayout''\n\n  export default new RootLayout((scope) => {\n    const baseAtoms = useAtoms()\n\n    const partners = new Stream({\n      fn: apiGetPartners,\n      queryKey: ''partners'',\n      atom: [baseAtoms, ''partners'']\n    })\n\n    return <>\n      <Nav />\n\n      <Show when={scope.Location().pathname === ''/''}>\n        <Hero />\n      </Show>\n\n      <div class="post">\n        <aside classList={{ visible: baseAtoms.store.isSidenavVisible }}>\n          <Posts />\n          <OnThisPage baseAtoms={baseAtoms} />\n\n          <section class="unity">\n            <Collaborate />\n            <Partners partners={partners} />\n          </section>\n        </aside>\n\n        <main>{scope.childrenRootLayout}</main>\n      </div>\n\n      <Footer />\n\n      <Search baseAtoms={baseAtoms} />\n    </>\n  })\n  ```\n\n\n\n## SubLayouts\n- Sometimes it''d be lovely to give a collection of `Routes` the same `data` and/or `design` and this is what `SubLayouts` are perfect for\n- Create a Sub Layout:\n  ```ts\n  import { SubLayout } from ''@ace/subLayout''\n\n  export default new SubLayout((scope) => {\n    return <>\n      <h1>Example</h1>\n      <main>{scope.childrenSubLayout}</main>\n    </>\n  })\n  ```\n- Place a Route w/in a `SubLayout`:\n  ```ts\n  import ExampleLayout from ''@src/app/ExampleLayout''\n\n  export default new Route(''/dashboard'')\n    .layout(ExampleLayout)\n    .component((scope) => {\n      return <div>Dashboard</div>\n    })\n  ```\n\n\n## Routes Directory\n- Ace is built ontop of [Solid Start](https://docs.solidjs.com/solid-start/)\n- Solid Start uses [File Based Routing](https://docs.solidjs.com/solid-start/building-your-application/routing)\n- During `ace build <env>` we take care of placing routes and layouts into the `./src/routes` folder for you!\n- This way when you want to alter what routes are in what layouts, there is no need to ensure you place the proper files/folders correctly. Just declare `.layout(ExampleLayout)` & Ace has it from there!\n','\n',char(10)),4,1766642649000);
INSERT INTO posts VALUES(16,'Routes','routes',5,replace('# Routes\n- In the `ace.config.js` we specify an `app` directory\n- During `ace build <env>`, Ace will search the `app` directory for any routes\n- Every Route is `automatically` placed into the [RootLayout](/post/layouts#rootlayout)\n- With Ace there are 2 types of routes. `Route` & `Route404`\n\n\n\n## Route\n```ts\nimport { Route } from ''@ace/route''\nimport { Title } from ''@solidjs/meta''\n\n\nexport default new Route(''/'')\n  .component(() => {\n    return <>\n      <Title>🏡 Home · Ace Docs!</Title>\n      <h1> Aloha</h1>\n    </>\n  })\n```\n\n\n\n## Route404\n```ts\n// The NotFound.tsx we use for this site :)\n\nimport ''./NotFound.css''\nimport { A } from ''@ace/a''\nimport { Tron } from ''@ace/tron''\nimport { Lottie } from ''@ace/lottie''\nimport { Title } from ''@solidjs/meta''\nimport { Route404 } from ''@ace/route404''\n\n\nexport default new Route404()\n  .component((scope) => {\n    return <>\n      <Title>😅 404</Title>\n\n      <main class="not-found">\n        <Lottie src="/lottie/not-found.lottie" />\n        <div class="message">We don''t have a page called:</div>\n        <div class="path">{scope.location.pathname}</div>\n\n        <Tron $div={{ class: ''brand'' }} color="var(--ace-primary)">\n          <A path="/" $a={{ class: ''brand'' }}>🏡 Go Back Home</A>\n        </Tron>\n      </main>\n    </>\n  })\n```\n\n\n\n## SubLayouts\n- Sometimes it''d be lovely to give a collection of `Routes` the same `data` and/or `design` and this is what [`SubLayouts`](/post/layouts#sublayouts) are perfect for\n  ```ts\n  import { Route } from ''@ace/route''\n  import { Title } from ''@solidjs/meta''\n  import DashboardLayout from ''./DashboardLayout''\n\n\n  export default new Route(''/'')\n    .layout(DashboardLayout) // ❤️ SubLayout!\n    .component(() => {\n      return <>\n        <Title>🏡 Home · Ace Docs!</Title>\n        <h1> Aloha</h1>\n      </>\n    })\n  ```\n\n\n\n## Routes Directory\n- Ace is built ontop of [Solid Start](https://docs.solidjs.com/solid-start/)\n- Solid Start uses [File Based Routing](https://docs.solidjs.com/solid-start/building-your-application/routing)\n- During `ace build <env>` we take care of placing routes and layouts into the `./src/routes` folder for you!\n- This way when you want to alter what routes are in what layouts, there is no need to ensure you place the proper files/folders correctly. Just declare `.layout(ExampleLayout)` & Ace has it from there!\n\n\n\n## Path Params\n- When defined in the path string, path params always start w/ a colon\n- There are 2 types of path params, `Required` & `Optional`\n- `Required` path params do `NOT` have a question mark at the end\n- `Optional` path params `HAVE` a question mark at the end\n- Example:\n  ```ts\n  export default new Route(''/example/:slug/:mode?'')\n  ```\n- If you would love to validate the types for these path params use a parser. A `parser` is a `function` that takes an `input`, `throws` if the `input` is `invalid` and provides `parsed` data if all is `valid`. Ace has first class support for [Valibot](https://valibot.dev/guides/comparison/), we highly recommend the Valibot [plugin](/post/ace-config#plugins), but custom parser functions, Zod or any parsing library works gr8 too, Valibot example:\n  ```ts\n  import { Route } from ''@ace/route''\n  import { optional, vNum, vBool, vParser } from ''@ace/vParser''\n\n  export default new Route(''/example/:slug/:mode?'')\n    .parser(vParser.route({\n      pathParams: {\n        slug: vNum(),\n        mode: optional(vBool()),\n      }\n    }))\n    .component((scope) => {\n      return <>\n        <h1>{scope.pathParams.slug}</h1>\n        <div>{scope.pathParams.mode}</div>\n      </>\n    })\n  ```\n- ❤️ Type Safe!\n![Ace Route Path Params](https://i.imgur.com/g6xQhaQ.png)\n\n\n\n## Search Params\n- Defining route search params requires no additions to the path string but does require a parser. A `parser` is a `function` that takes an `input`, `throws` if the `input` is `invalid` and provides `parsed` data if all is `valid`. Ace has first class support for [Valibot](https://valibot.dev/guides/comparison/), we highly recommend the Valibot [plugin](/post/ace-config#plugins), but custom parser functions, Zod or any parsing library works gr8 too, Valibot example:\n  ```ts\n  import { Route } from ''@ace/route''\n  import { optional, vNum, vBool, vParser } from ''@ace/vParser''\n\n  export default new Route(''/example/:slug/:mode?'')\n    .parser(vParser.route({\n      searchParams: {\n        slug: vNum(),\n        mode: optional(vBool()),\n      }\n    }))\n    .component((scope) => {\n      return <>\n        <h1>{scope.searchParams.slug}</h1>\n        <div>{scope.searchParams.mode}</div>\n      </>\n    })\n  ```\n- ❤️ Type Safe!\n![Ace Route Search Params](https://i.imgur.com/LDYUSdI.png)\n','\n',char(10)),4,1766705942000);
